><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20251113111620+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="393a894e-9090-41d1-ad47-b8b5e55f064e" date="D:20251113111620+10'00'" name="224912e0-fa70-44b3-908a-6cb08bfd4993" icon="Comment" page="3" rect="376.530060,220.862350,400.530060,244.862350" subject="Sticky Note" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>1. Training in the heat should be chosen over cold altitude&#xD;environments if the goal is to optimize athletes’ hematological&#xD;profiles.&#xD;2. Heat training adaptations are evident in Melbourne during&#xD;preseason spring/summer months. Therefore, AF teams (and&#xD;other Australian winter team sports) may not achieve additional&#xD;benefits traveling to warmer climates during this&#xD;period.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="3" rect="612.000000,152.862366,796.000000,244.862366"
/></text
